<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
    <artifactId>cometgui-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>cometgui-app</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>
  <name>CometGUI :: Application</name>
  <description>The assembled application: JavaFX bootstrap, dependency injection and configuration.</description>

  <properties>
    <!--
      HEADLESS JAVAFX, REPLICATED FROM cometgui-ui/pom.xml ON PURPOSE.

      This block is a near-copy of the one in cometgui-ui/pom.xml, and the
      duplication is deliberate rather than an oversight.  The Monocle jar is
      copied by maven-dependency-plugin into THIS module's own
      target/monocle/, so the patch-module path below is module-relative; a
      parent-level argLine naming a path that exists in only two of twelve
      modules would break every other module's test run, which is worse than
      two copies that a reader can diff.  If you change one, change the other
      and say so in the phase handoff.

      WHAT EACH PART IS FOR (the long version is in cometgui-ui/pom.xml and in
      docs/feasibility/gui-automation-spike.rst, "The JVM options TestFX needs
      on JDK 25"):

      patch-module: the pinned Liberica JDK bundles JavaFX 25.0.4+1 but its
      javafx.graphics contains no Monocle, and this host has no libX11, so the
      GTK platform cannot load libglass.so.  org.testfx:openjfx-monocle
      supplies a headless Glass platform.  It is patched INTO javafx.graphics
      because that is a named system module whose PlatformFactory lookup cannot
      see the unnamed module.

      The add-exports/add-opens pairs are the NINE options the feasibility
      document lists, kept WHOLE rather than trimmed to what today's tests
      happen to need: the next work unit in this phase adds TestFX here, and
      TestFX 4.0.18 reflectively instantiates MonoclePlatformFactory and
      HeadlessPlatform and writes them into private static fields.  Trimming
      the set now would only mean rediscovering it later from a stack trace.

      ONE OPTION cometgui-ui DOES NOT HAVE.  Written here without its two
      leading dashes, because an XML comment may not contain them; the real
      option is in the property below.

      enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED.  This module's
      org.cometgui.app.config.FfmGlibcVersionSource calls
      gnu_get_libc_version through java.lang.foreign, and on JDK 25 a
      restricted method called from the unnamed module (the class path) prints
      a WARNING unless native access is enabled for it.  The javafx.graphics
      entry beside it is what Monocle itself needs; the two are separate
      grants and both are required.  The packaged application of phase 16
      needs the same option in its jpackage java-options.

      LD_LIBRARY_PATH, FONTCONFIG_PATH, XDG_DATA_HOME and XDG_CACHE_HOME (in
      the surefire block below) point at the project-local font stack.  Without
      them a Scene containing any Control dies with "fontFactory is null".

      PLATFORM SCOPE, stated honestly: this is the Linux headless recipe and
      the library directory is amd64-specific, exactly as in cometgui-ui.  The
      phase that adds macOS and Windows runners owns splitting it into
      OS-activated profiles.
    -->
    <cometgui.monocleJar>${project.build.directory}/monocle/openjfx-monocle.jar</cometgui.monocleJar>
    <cometgui.fontstackRoot>${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}/tools/fontstack-bookworm-20260829/root</cometgui.fontstackRoot>
    <cometgui.surefire.extraArgLine>
      --patch-module javafx.graphics=${cometgui.monocleJar}
      --add-exports javafx.graphics/com.sun.javafx.tk=ALL-UNNAMED
      --add-exports javafx.graphics/com.sun.glass.ui=ALL-UNNAMED
      --add-opens javafx.graphics/com.sun.glass.ui=ALL-UNNAMED
      --add-exports javafx.graphics/com.sun.glass.ui.monocle=ALL-UNNAMED
      --add-opens javafx.graphics/com.sun.glass.ui.monocle=ALL-UNNAMED
      --add-exports javafx.graphics/com.sun.javafx.application=ALL-UNNAMED
      --add-opens javafx.graphics/com.sun.javafx.application=ALL-UNNAMED
      --enable-native-access=javafx.graphics
      --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED
      -Dglass.platform=Monocle
      -Dmonocle.platform=Headless
      -Dprism.order=sw
      -Djava.awt.headless=true
    </cometgui.surefire.extraArgLine>
  </properties>

  <!--
    Only the edges the specification's layering permits are declared here.
    Adding one is an architectural change: say so in the phase handoff, and
    expect the ArchUnit rules in cometgui-archtests to have an opinion.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-domain</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-provenance</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-process</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-tools</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-install</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-params-comet</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-params-percolator</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-results</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-workflow</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      AtlantaFX (phase 02): the JavaFX theme stylesheets the CasanovoGUI base
      uses, driven from org.cometgui.app.config.derived.AtlantaFxThemes.  The
      version is pinned in the parent's <dependencyManagement>.

      THE EXCLUSION IS NOT OPTIONAL.  atlantafx-base declares
      org.openjfx:javafx-controls at COMPILE scope.  Without the exclusion
      `mvn -pl cometgui-app dependency:tree` reports six extra artefacts:

          io.github.mkpaz:atlantafx-base:jar:2.1.0:compile
          \- org.openjfx:javafx-controls:jar:23:compile
             +- org.openjfx:javafx-controls:jar:linux:23:compile
             \- org.openjfx:javafx-graphics:jar:23:compile
                +- org.openjfx:javafx-graphics:jar:linux:23:compile
                \- org.openjfx:javafx-base:jar:23:compile
                   \- org.openjfx:javafx-base:jar:linux:23:compile

      This project has NO org.openjfx dependency anywhere: JavaFX 25.0.4+1
      comes from the pinned Liberica Full JDK as resolved system modules, and a
      second (here: JavaFX 23) copy of the javafx.* packages on the class path
      is exactly the breakage the parent POM's opening comment forbids.

      JUNIT DOES NOT NEED EXCLUDING, checked rather than assumed.
      atlantafx-base also declares junit-jupiter-api, junit-jupiter-engine and
      assertj-core, but its own parent manages all three to <scope>test</scope>,
      so none of them is transitive and none appears on this module's tree.  An
      exclusion for them would be inert; if a future AtlantaFX changes that, the
      tree will show it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.mkpaz</groupId>
      <artifactId>atlantafx-base</artifactId>
      <exclusions>
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
          <artifactId>*</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      TestFX (phase 02, unit 8), the robot behind
      org.cometgui.app.uidriver.TestFxUiDriver.  TEST SCOPE, here and in the
      parent's <dependencyManagement>, and declared in no other module: it is
      test machinery and must never reach a published artefact (R-TEST-06).
      Phase 00's spike proved 4.0.18 against this JDK and JavaFX pair; the nine
      JVM options it needs are already in this POM's argLine.

      testfx-core and testfx-junit5 declare EUPL 1.1 in their POMs and pull
      org.hamcrest:hamcrest, org.assertj:assertj-core and org.osgi:org.osgi.core
      at RUNTIME scope, which Maven narrows to test scope here.  None is
      redistributed with CometGUI; the licence audit is told so in the phase
      handoff rather than being left to discover it.

      NO EXCLUSION IS NEEDED, checked rather than assumed: testfx-core declares
      org.testfx:openjfx-monocle only at test scope, so it is not transitive and
      the Monocle jar this module patches into javafx.graphics stays the one
      maven-dependency-plugin copies below.  `mvn -pl cometgui-app
      dependency:tree` must keep showing no org.openjfx artefact and no
      openjfx-monocle on the class path.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>testfx-core</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
      <exclusions>
        <!--
          THE ASSERTJ EXCLUSION IS NOT OPTIONAL, and it is not a way of
          quietening a gate.

          testfx-core declares org.assertj:assertj-core 3.13.2 at RUNTIME
          scope, which Maven narrows to test scope here.  That version carries
          GHSA-rqfh-9r24-8c9r / CVE-2026-24400 (XML external entity processing
          in `isXmlEqualTo`), and scripts/ci/dependency-scan.py fails the
          supplychain stage on it, correctly.

          Excluding it REMOVES the component rather than accepting it.  AssertJ
          is used by exactly one part of TestFX, its optional assertion API:
          `javap` over the 4.0.18 jar shows references to org/assertj only from
          org/testfx/assertions/**, and nothing in this project imports that
          package.  The FxUiDriver implementations use org.testfx.api.FxRobot
          and JUnit's own assertions.  If that ever stops being true the tests
          fail with NoClassDefFoundError, loudly, which is the right failure.

          The alternatives were worse: an allowlist entry accepts a known
          advisory for no benefit, and managing assertj-core to a fixed version
          would add a dependency this project does not otherwise have.

          org.hamcrest:hamcrest and org.osgi:org.osgi.core come the same way and
          are NOT excluded: neither carries an advisory today, and TestFX's
          matcher API and its OSGi activator are part of the library proper.
        -->
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
          <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>testfx-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
      <exclusions>
        <!--
          The same exclusion, for the same reason, on the other coordinate that
          brings assertj-core in: see the block above.  Checked rather than
          assumed here too, `javap` over testfx-junit5 4.0.18 shows no class in
          it referencing org/assertj at all.
        -->
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
          <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        Monocle is fetched into THIS module's build directory and injected with
        patch-module; it is deliberately NOT a <dependency>, because anything on
        the test class path lands in the unnamed module where javafx.graphics
        cannot see it.  The version is pinned in the parent POM.  cometgui-ui
        has the same execution over the same artefact into its own target/.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>fetch-monocle</id>
            <phase>generate-test-resources</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>copy</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <artifactItems>
                <artifactItem>
                  <groupId>org.testfx</groupId>
                  <artifactId>openjfx-monocle</artifactId>
                  <version>${openjfx-monocle.version}</version>
                  <type>jar</type>
                  <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}/monocle</outputDirectory>
                  <destFileName>openjfx-monocle.jar</destFileName>
                </artifactItem>
              </artifactItems>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <!--
            One JVM per test class.  Phase 00 found that a shared fork lets one
            toolkit-owning test break the next one's robot input; isolating the
            JVMs is the fix, and relaxing the assertion would not have been.
            It matters twice over here: the startup smoke test calls
            Application.launch, which may be called at most once in a JVM.
          -->
          <reuseForks>false</reuseForks>
          <!--
            The forked JVM's environment, not system properties: the dynamic
            linker and fontconfig read these at process start, so setting them
            with -D would be too late and would do nothing.
          -->
          <environmentVariables>
            <LD_LIBRARY_PATH>${cometgui.fontstackRoot}/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu</LD_LIBRARY_PATH>
            <FONTCONFIG_PATH>${cometgui.fontstackRoot}/etc/fonts</FONTCONFIG_PATH>
            <XDG_DATA_HOME>${cometgui.fontstackRoot}/usr/share</XDG_DATA_HOME>
            <!-- fontconfig's cache belongs in the build directory, not in the
                 developer's home: no test may depend on home-directory state. -->
            <XDG_CACHE_HOME>${project.build.directory}/fontconfig-cache</XDG_CACHE_HOME>
          </environmentVariables>
          <systemPropertyVariables>
            <cometgui.fontstackRoot>${cometgui.fontstackRoot}</cometgui.fontstackRoot>
          </systemPropertyVariables>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
